  "slug": "segmentation-segment-8349",
  "summary": "Five preprints present tools for segmenting, reconstructing and aligning biological structures in 2D and 3D across scales, from nuclei to connectomes.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is computational delineation of biological structures in volumetric or multi-section data, with a growing emphasis on tractable, semi-automated workflows. EpiCycle uses human-in-the-loop iteration for 3D segmentation of stratified epithelia in zebrafish skin, mouse salivary glands and human fetal tissue. A Vaa3D-based platform combines semi-automatic neuronal nucleus segmentation with PCA-based shape analysis and synthetic simulation. A random-walk method probabilistically reconstructs microglia morphology and assigns voxels to cells of origin. Jump provides web tools for exploring and annotating public EM connectomics data such as MICrONS. A benchmark of 15 alignment algorithms for spatial transcriptomics finds no single best method across scenarios, so method choice remains task-dependent.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
